I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build and refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and validate that every submitted artifact comes from the saved final project. I’ll keep uncertain assignments or fractions explicitly qualified.

The chemistry points strongly toward zinc tungstate formation, but I won’t assume a single-phase product: residual ZnO/WO₃ or a secondary zinc tungstate phase can materially affect the quantitative result. I’m now extracting the peak set and checking the available reference/search and GSAS-II interfaces.

The full peak sequence is consistent with monoclinic wolframite-type ZnWO₄, including the dense high-angle multiplets. Several low-intensity features overlap where residual ZnO or monoclinic WO₃ would appear, so I’ll test one-, two-, and three-phase refinements rather than assigning them from peak positions alone. The observed peaks are moderately broad, which also makes trace-phase claims less secure.

The single-phase trial has already refined the ZnWO₄ cell to a substantially better positional match, but its profile residual remains high enough that phase-model testing is justified. One nuisance is a redundant overall/phase scale in the initial script; I’m correcting that for stable quantitative comparisons before accepting any fraction.

The stabilized refinements are still running across four nested models. They now use Poisson counting weights, a fixed common histogram scale, phase-specific broadening, refined cells, and the reported Cu Kα doublet; this removes the exact scale singularity and makes the phase-fraction comparison meaningful.

The model grid is computationally slower than the initial test because each candidate now carries independent crystallite-size/microstrain terms. I’m letting it complete rather than truncating a comparison mid-refinement; after this pass I’ll retain only statistically supported phases and simplify any over-parameterized broadening.

No user action is needed; the job is active rather than hung, but output is being logged to avoid flooding this thread. The decisive checks will be whether added-phase scales remain positive with sensible cells and whether their characteristic non-overlapping peaks reduce the difference curve, not merely whether Rwp drops.

The model comparison is decisive for monoclinic WO₃: adding it drops Rwp from about 19.5% to 14.7% and accounts for independent peaks near 23.2, 26.7, 29.0, and 33–34°. ZnO is not supported; its unconstrained scale becomes unstable or collapses when WO₃ is present. I’m now rebuilding the accepted two-phase model without the trial’s nonphysical microstrain excursion, then I’ll test whether any ZnO signal remains against that stable baseline.

A controlled ZnO addition against the fixed two-phase baseline is positive and significant: its refined scale corresponds to roughly 8 wt% and lowers Rwp from 14.77% to 14.34%, with contributions at the wurtzite peak sequence rather than only under one overlap. I’m treating that as provisional until its cell and broadening refine to sensible values in a full three-phase pass.

The unrestricted oxygen-coordinate trial lowers Rwp, but it also produces implausibly short Zn–O contacts, so I’m rejecting that apparent improvement as overfitting. I’m limiting any structural-coordinate refinement to heavy-atom positions that the X-ray data can actually constrain; oxygen frameworks will remain tied to credible reference structures unless a restrained result stays chemically sensible.

The alternative neutron-derived WO₃ model gives essentially the same phase fraction and fit but has a complete, chemically sensible WO₆ network. I’m keeping that model and converting it to an equivalent reduced monoclinic setting (β near 91°) before the final refinement, so the submitted CIF is both chemically robust and straightforward to interpret.

The accepted structural strategy is now fixed: reduced-setting monoclinic WO₃ from the neutron model, literature oxygen frameworks, refined unit cells and crystallite broadening for all phases, refined heavy-atom positions where supported, and a mild [100] March–Dollase correction for the dominant ZnWO₄. I’m running the final joint refinement over the full measured range, then exporting every deliverable from a reload of that saved project.

The final joint refinement converged without singularities. The modeled crystalline fractions are now about 77.3 wt% ZnWO₄, 16.2 wt% monoclinic WO₃, and 6.6 wt% wurtzite ZnO; Rwp is 14.04% over all 8,301 points. I’m doing final cross-checks now: CIF parse/stoichiometry and bond geometry, fraction normalization, profile reproducibility from `final.gpx`, and file/schema integrity.

The final physically constrained three-phase refinement is ready. I tested a lower-residual ADP refinement, but it drove every refined displacement parameter strongly negative, so I rejected that overfit and retained the chemically plausible model for the submitted project and CIFs.

Validation confirms that `final.gpx`, all three CIFs, and the 8,301-point profile export are synchronized. The final refinement converged with 41 variables and a maximum shift/esd of 0.011; I’m now recording the quantitative result and the limitations that keep the formal uncertainties from being overinterpreted.

One systematic feature remains in the residual: calculated high-angle intensities decay faster than observed. Because the metadata explicitly leaves effective polarization and the instrumental profile uncalibrated, I’m checking whether a physically bounded polarization/profile correction explains that trend before freezing the quantitative fractions.

The polarization trial is computationally slower than the earlier refinements but is still progressing. I’m keeping it isolated from `final.gpx`; no submitted artifact is being changed during these checks.

The free-polarization fit lowered the residual but overshot the physical range (1.49) and destabilized two profile-mixing terms, so it is not an acceptable model. I’m checking the conservative boundary case—non-negative displacement parameters—because that directly tests whether the high-angle trend can be resolved without an unphysical parameter.

The boundary tests confirm the phase fractions are fairly stable, but the residual can be lowered only by forcing zero displacement or super-physical polarization/profile values. I’m retaining the converged, chemically plausible refinement and will explicitly label the formal errors as optimistic; the realistic compositional precision is about 1–2 absolute wt%.

All requested artifacts have now been written. I’m performing the final cross-check against the saved GSAS-II project—fractions, residuals, profile rows, CIF paths, convergence, and JSON validity—so the handoff values cannot drift from `final.gpx`.

